<ContentPage
  title="What is an intent-based DEX aggregator? | Ophis"
  description="An intent-based DEX aggregator lets you sign the outcome you want instead of submitting a swap transaction: solvers compete to fill your signed order, batch-settled with MEV protection."
  path="/learn/intent-based-dex-aggregator/"
  heading="What is an intent-based DEX aggregator?"
  lede="You sign the outcome you want, a solver competition executes it. Here is how intents differ from routers and RFQs, and why batch auctions change the trader's position."
  updated={updated}
  faqs={FAQS}
>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> an intent-based DEX aggregator lets you sign what you want
    (sell this token, receive at least that amount) instead of submitting a routed swap transaction
    yourself. Competing solvers execute the signed order, pay the gas, and cannot fill it below
    your signed limit. Uniform clearing prices per token pair and offchain submission mitigate
    common MEV; settlement itself can still be public. Ophis is an intent-based aggregator built on CoW Protocol's
    batch-auction design, extended for autonomous agents.
  </p>

  {FAQS.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{f.q}</h2>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>How Ophis implements this</h2>
  <p>
    Ophis adds a natural-language intent layer (describe the trade, the parser fills the order), a
    public <a href="https://docs.ophis.fi/ai-agents">MCP server</a> so autonomous agents can trade
    with their own keys, and the <a href="https://github.com/ophis-fi/ophis/tree/main/packages/sdk">@ophis/sdk</a>
    safety helpers for building bounded orders. Fees are on the
    <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>, the chain list on
    <a href="/supported-chains/">supported chains</a>, and the custody and audit detail on the
    <a href="/security/">security page</a>. For the MEV mechanics in depth, read
    <a href="/learn/mev-protected-swaps/">MEV-protected swaps, explained</a>.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
